# For more information on these fields, see:
# https://chromium.googlesource.com/chromium/src/+/HEAD/docs/adding_to_third_party.md#readme_chromium
#
# TaffyGo vendors CPython under //taffy rather than under Chromium's own
# //third_party because the interpreter is a TaffyGo product decision, not an
# upstream one: decision 0046 records why the interpreter ships inside the
# installer while its standard library arrives through the asset plane, and
# nothing in the Chromium tree is edited to make that true.
#
# No CPython source is committed here. The pin in tools/manifest.json names the
# release tarball and the SHA-256 the release's own SPDX document records, and
# tools/build_interpreter.py cross-builds it with the Chromium checkout's clang
# and the NDK sysroot -- the same toolchain that will link the result. A source
# tree of this size is a checkout-time dependency, and committing a second copy
# of it would be the kind of duplicate this repository treats as a defect.
# Decision 0067 settles where it arrives: ./tools/chromium/sync unpacks the
# pinned tarball at src/ after checking it against that digest, and //taffy is
# already a symlink to this repository, so src/ is one directory the checkout
# and the tree both see.
#
# WHY "License File" NAMES A PATH A BARE CLONE DOES NOT CARRY. PSF-2.0 is
# CPython's licence and LICENSE is its member of the pinned tarball, which
# tools/manifest.json records as licence_member. That member unpacks with the
# rest of the tree, so src/LICENSE is where the licence text is in a synced
# checkout and nowhere at all in a bare clone. Sixty-five README.chromium files
# in the Chromium tree at the pinned revision are in exactly that state, naming
# a licence below a src/ subdirectory that only a sync populates; libvpx names
# source/libvpx/LICENSE for the same reason. This field named a bare LICENSE
# until 2026-08-26 and no such file was ever committed here -- decision 0065
# recorded the gap and this is the correction. Copying the text in beside these
# files was refused for two independent reasons: it would be a second copy of a
# file the mount already carries, which is the duplication the paragraph above
# refuses for the source itself and which is free to drift from it; and the
# text is release-specific, so a copy taken from any tree other than the one
# manifest.json pins is a claim about a release it did not come from.
#
# The build is not a stock CPython. Every extension module is compiled in,
# because a sandboxed utility process cannot dlopen; _ctypes and _socket are
# configured out, because the sandbox has neither native calls nor network; and
# every loadable segment is aligned to 16 KiB, because an Android device with
# that page size will not load a binary aligned for 4 KiB. build_interpreter.py
# refuses a build that broke any of the three rather than reporting a size for
# it.

Name: CPython
Short Name: cpython
URL: https://www.python.org/ftp/python/3.14.7/Python-3.14.7.tar.xz
Version: 3.14.7
Update Mechanism: Manual
License: PSF-2.0
License File: LICENSE
Shipped: yes
Security Critical: yes
CPEPrefix: cpe:/a:python:python:3.14.7

Description:
The interpreter for the sandboxed Python utility service decision 0007
describes and spike SP-07 gates. GN compiles the pinned source directly for
android-arm64 and android-x64 with committed per-ABI configuration; the
shipping Android profiles link it only into the isolated utility worker.
The standard library remains a verified descriptor-backed asset.

Licence File:
The Python Software Foundation text is committed here as LICENSE rather than
referenced at src/LICENSE, which is where the interpreter tarball puts it.
`/taffy-core/third_party/cpython/src/` is gitignored — the source is fetched
at version-roll time, not committed — so a `License File: src/LICENSE` named a
path that existed on the machine that had built CPython and on no clone. The
copy here is byte-for-byte the 3.14.7 tarball's own file.

Local Modifications:
None to the source. `tools/build_interpreter.py` produces the reviewed ABI
configuration at version-roll time; `tools/prepare_gn_inputs.py` commits those
headers and the frozen bootstrap inputs. GN runs no configure step and consumes
no prebuilt Python archive. No patch is applied to any CPython file.
